From: VAP Memory Optimization <noreply@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH] cel: build varEnvs lazily instead of materializing all 8 combos

Today every `compiler` (one per `CompositedCompiler`, one per policy in the
absence of patch 0002) eagerly builds 8 EnvSets in `mustBuildEnvs` covering the
Cartesian product of {HasParams, HasAuthorizer, HasPatchTypes}. Each EnvSet is
two cel-go `cel.Env.Extend` clones, so the matrix costs 16 env clones per
compiler — independent of how many of those combos a given policy will actually
use.

In production:
  - VAP uses 2 of 8 combos (validations vs messageExpressions differ only on
    HasAuthorizer; HasPatchTypes is MAP-only).
  - MAP uses up to 4 of 8.

That's 75% wasted matrix capacity per unique compiler instance. After patches
0001/0002, this is the dominant residual `F` cost paid by policies that 0002
cannot collapse (i.e. those whose variable signatures differ).

This patch converts `varEnvs` to a lazy memoized table keyed by
OptionalVariableDeclarations, materialized on first use via sync.Once-per-key.
Semantics are unchanged: createEnvForOpts is pure with respect to its inputs,
so memoization is observationally invisible.

Expected savings: ~3/4 of per-compiler env-clone heap on the 0002-miss path.
Estimated 1–4 GiB at 10,000 unique-shape policies.

